+## Phase 6: User Story 4 - Hand the collection to another tool (Priority: P4)
+
+**Goal**: `export --format csv` prints the whole collection as CSV beginning with the exact header `id,url,title,tags`; `export --format json` prints it as a JSON array of objects; an empty store exports successfully; any other format is rejected with a stderr diagnostic and a non-zero status.
+
+**Independent Test**: With a known set of bookmarks — including one whose title contains a comma, a double quote and a newline, and one with non-ASCII text — export in each format and confirm `csv.DictReader` and `json.load` re-read every bookmark's id, URL, title and tags unchanged, in ascending id order with alphabetical tags. Then export an empty store in both formats (header line only / `[]`, both status 0) and request `--format xml` (stderr diagnostic, status 1).
+
+### Tests for User Story 4
+
+> **NOTE: Write these tests FIRST and confirm they FAIL before implementing T032–T034**
+
+- [ ] T031 [P] [US4] Write `tests/test_export.py` covering FR-019 (line 1 is exactly `id,url,title,tags`; one row per bookmark in ascending id order; the tags field holds the alphabetical comma-joined tags; values containing `,`, `"` or a line break round-trip through `csv.reader`/`csv.DictReader` as the original values), FR-020 (`json.loads` of stdout gives a list of objects with exactly the keys `id`, `url`, `title`, `tags`, with `id` a JSON number and `tags` an alphabetically sorted array that is `[]` when the bookmark has no tags), FR-021 (`--format xml` and an omitted `--format` each write a diagnostic to stderr and exit non-zero — status 1 per contracts/cli.md), FR-022 (an empty store prints the header line alone for CSV and `[]` for JSON, both exiting 0), and FR-023 (non-ASCII URL, title and tags are emitted literally, not as `\uXXXX`, and re-read identically from both formats)
+
+### Implementation for User Story 4
